  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday morning through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 213 PM EDT Thu Oct 1 2026\n\nA marked increase in moisture is expected Saturday through \nMonday, with the potential for heavy rainfall and localized flash \nflooding. PWAT upwards of 2 inches per ensemble guidance (above \nthe daily maximum per sounding climo) and favorable jet stream \ndynamics for ascent are two ingredients that will be in place. \nThat said, much uncertainty remains regarding where the heaviest \nrainfall and flash flooding risk will be most likely, owing to \nfactors including the strength of a cold air damming (CAD) \"wedge\"\nand how far north moisture advection is maximized. Severe weather\nis very unlikely given a pattern that limits instability.\n\nRainfall Uncertainty: Per the 07z National Blend of Models (NBM),\nthe spread between the 10th and 90th percentiles for total \nrainfall from Saturday morning through Monday morning is 2.75-3.75\ninches. This spread is indicative of uncertainty in where exactly\nthe heaviest rainfall will occur this weekend into Monday, and/or\nhow much. To illustrate this -- the latest GEFS places the swath \nof greatest rainfall across north Georgia, the EPS across central \nGeorgia, and the CMCE across central Georgia (with totals greater \nthan the EPS, but similar to the GEFS). A few CAMs (the NAM and a \ncouple versions of the RRFS) extend into the Saturday/Sunday time \nframe and depict the isolated nature of maximized rainfall totals.\nThus, maintaining our messaging for localized areas of 3-4 inches\nseems prudent, with the potential for 5+ inches as a reasonable \n\"worst case scenario\" in isolated locales.\n\nThe Wedge: The strength of high pressure over the Northeast and \nsoutheastern Canada will largely govern the strength of the \nresulting CAD wedge across the southern Appalachians. The global \nmodels struggle to resolve the wedge and thus have a tendency to \nunderestimate its strength and erode it too quickly. Where exactly\nthe wedge is situated will have an impact on rainfall totals, as \nthe boundary of the CAD airmass acts as a front and may ramp up \nshower/storm development, intensity, and longevity. Assuming at \nleast some semblance of a wedge and thus increased clouds and \nprecip, high temperatures on Sunday and Monday should be capped in\nthe 70s and lower 80s (with 60s in the mountains).\n\nAn expansive high pressure system will spread across much of the \neastern CONUS Tuesday and Wednesday, shunting moisture southward. \nWhere exactly the boundary between the dry airmass of the high \npressure and moisture south of the high ends up is uncertain, and \nwill govern where PoPs are focused both days. The GEFS suggests \nthat some moisture will remain across north and central Georgia, \nwhile the EPS suggests that moisture will be relegated to south \nGeorgia. For fans of autumn -- highs on Tuesday and Wednesday will\nbe in the upper 60s to mid-70s for the most part, and lows will \nbe in the 50s (with 40s in the mountains).",
